---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/omega-3`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Omega-3 Fatty Acids Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Omega-3', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Omega-3 (EPA & DHA): Cardiovascular Evidence, ALA & Algae Sources"
  description="Complete omega-3 reference — EPA and DHA needs, fish oil vs algae, cardiovascular RCTs (REDUCE-IT, STRENGTH, VITAL), triglyceride reduction, and vegan sources."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/omega-3", label: "Omega-3" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Omega-3 Fatty Acids</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Omega-3s are essential polyunsaturated fatty acids the body cannot make. Three matter:
      short-chain <strong>ALA</strong> (α-linolenic acid, from plants) and long-chain
      <strong>EPA</strong> and <strong>DHA</strong> (from marine sources). Only long-chain
      forms have robust clinical evidence — ALA conversion to EPA/DHA is inefficient (~5–10%).
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>ALA (adequate intake):</strong> 1.6 g/day men, 1.1 g/day women.</li>
      <li><strong>EPA + DHA:</strong> no formal RDA; expert consensus 250–500 mg/day combined for cardiovascular health, 1,000+ mg for established heart disease.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy / lactation:</strong> at least 200–300 mg DHA/day for fetal neurodevelopment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cardiovascular evidence</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
      The largest recent trials have produced mixed results:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>REDUCE-IT</strong> (2019) — icosapent ethyl (highly purified EPA) 4 g/day cut major cardiovascular events by 25% in high-risk patients on statins.</li>
      <li><strong>STRENGTH</strong> (2020) — mixed EPA+DHA at 4 g/day showed no benefit vs corn oil placebo. The EPA-only formulation, not "fish oil in general," appears to drive the REDUCE-IT signal.</li>
      <li><strong>VITAL</strong> (2019) — 1 g/day of standard fish oil in the general population showed no reduction in major cardiovascular events, small reduction in myocardial infarction.</li>
      <li><strong>Standard OTC fish oil (1 g/day)</strong> — modest triglyceride reduction (~10–15%), unclear clinical event impact for primary prevention.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mt-3">
      For post-MI or high triglyceride patients, prescription EPA (icosapent ethyl) is the
      evidence-based choice. For general prevention, eating fatty fish twice a week is more
      supported than a supplement.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Best sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Salmon, farmed (3 oz): 1,800 mg EPA+DHA</li>
      <li>Mackerel, Atlantic (3 oz): 1,000 mg</li>
      <li>Sardines (3 oz): 840 mg</li>
      <li>Anchovies (3 oz): 950 mg</li>
      <li>Herring (3 oz): 1,700 mg</li>
      <li>Flaxseed (1 tbsp ground): 2,400 mg ALA (~120–240 mg EPA/DHA equivalent after conversion)</li>
      <li>Chia seeds (1 oz): 5,000 mg ALA</li>
      <li>Walnuts (1 oz): 2,500 mg ALA</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fish oil vs algae oil</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Algae oil provides direct EPA and DHA — fish get their omega-3s by eating algae in the
      first place. Algae oil is vegan, cleaner (no methylmercury or persistent-pollutant
      concerns), and equally effective. Cost has fallen; it's a legitimate substitute for
      fish oil.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Fish oil supplement quality</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Read the label: look for combined EPA+DHA per serving, not total "fish oil" mass. A 1,000 mg fish oil capsule typically has only 300 mg EPA+DHA.</li>
      <li>Freshness matters — rancid fish oil (fishy burps, off smell) is common and may be harmful. Cold-store after opening.</li>
      <li>Third-party tested for oxidation and heavy metals (IFOS, USP) is worth the small premium.</li>
      <li>Triglyceride and re-esterified triglyceride forms absorb better than ethyl ester forms.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> High-dose omega-3 (&gt; 3 g/day) modestly increases
      bleeding time — discuss with your prescriber if you take anticoagulants. Some studies
      have flagged a small atrial fibrillation risk at high doses; the clinical significance
      is debated.
    </div>

    <EndOfContentAd />
    <RelatedArticles current="/omega-3" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
